WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.516
The marketing team has an agent that turns a brief into a published post.

00:00:06.816 --> 00:00:11.615
It researches the topic, writes the draft, reviews it against the brand guide, and

00:00:11.615 --> 00:00:13.078
publishes it to the blog.

00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:17.404
All in the same loop, with the same context, and the same tools.

00:00:18.054 --> 00:00:21.456
Here's how it was configured, more or less word for word.

00:00:21.756 --> 00:00:23.564
You are the content agent.

00:00:23.864 --> 00:00:28.783
Research, write, review rigorously, and publish only when it's excellent.

00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:30.409
And the tools.

00:00:30.709 --> 00:00:36.847
Web search, fetch a page, read the brand guide, write and edit the draft, check

00:00:36.847 --> 00:00:37.702
plagiarism.

00:00:38.002 --> 00:00:43.354
And also publish the post, post to social media, update the CRM, and send the

00:00:43.354 --> 00:00:44.118
newsletter.

00:00:44.768 --> 00:00:50.376
One Friday, the agent published a draft with a competitor's figures, copied from a

00:00:50.376 --> 00:00:52.360
page it read during research.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.655
The review step was in the prompt.

00:00:54.955 --> 00:01:00.326
But it was the same model, with the same context full of web pages, reviewing its

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:04.304
own text, with the publish tool within reach the whole time.

00:01:04.604 --> 00:01:09.250
In this lesson, you'll learn to see this smell, and where to make the cut.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:11.271
Here's the shape.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.633
The whole task goes to one agent.

00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:18.889
It holds every tool, and a prompt that tries to describe every role.

00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:25.690
Untrusted content from the research enters the same context that will decide whether

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:26.495
to publish.

00:01:27.145 --> 00:01:31.692
Whoever reviews is whoever wrote, looking at the same material.

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:35.974
And the side effect tool is available from the very first step.

00:01:36.624 --> 00:01:37.723
How do you spot it?

00:01:38.023 --> 00:01:40.572
One agent handles steps of different kinds.

00:01:40.872 --> 00:01:46.097
Research, production, review, and execution, all mixed together.

00:01:46.747 --> 00:01:51.290
Every tool, read and side effect alike, is available at every step.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.883
The system prompt describes several roles.

00:01:55.183 --> 00:01:58.230
You are researcher, writer, and reviewer.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.161
Review happens in the same context that produced the work.

00:02:03.461 --> 00:02:08.453
And by the end of the task, that context still carries all the raw material from the

00:02:08.453 --> 00:02:08.810
start.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:14.474
And a problem in one step forces changes to the prompt for all of them.

00:02:15.674 --> 00:02:16.723
It starts small.

00:02:17.023 --> 00:02:19.066
The agent only wrote drafts.

00:02:19.366 --> 00:02:21.501
Publishing was just one more tool.

00:02:22.151 --> 00:02:23.681
One agent looks simpler.

00:02:23.981 --> 00:02:26.408
One prompt, one loop, one deploy.

00:02:26.708 --> 00:02:29.317
Splitting looks like needless complexity.

00:02:29.967 --> 00:02:31.782
Good models cope in the demo.

00:02:32.082 --> 00:02:34.770
On short tasks, the single agent works fine.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.386
The problem only shows up at scale, or in odd cases.

00:02:39.036 --> 00:02:41.013
And nobody draws the boundaries.

00:02:41.313 --> 00:02:45.647
Without someone asking which steps really exist, they blend together.

00:02:46.847 --> 00:02:48.649
The first cost is quality.

00:02:48.949 --> 00:02:52.809
Review in the same context tends to agree with what was produced.

00:02:53.109 --> 00:02:57.169
The model rereads its own reasoning, not the result with fresh eyes.

00:02:57.819 --> 00:02:59.170
Then security.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:05.407
Untrusted content read during research sits in the same context as the side effect

00:03:05.407 --> 00:03:05.841
tools.

00:03:06.141 --> 00:03:12.069
That's the recipe for an attack where instructions hide inside the pages an agent

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:12.508
reads.

00:03:12.808 --> 00:03:18.002
A mega agent that researches and publishes in the same context is the classic

00:03:18.002 --> 00:03:18.474
target.

00:03:19.124 --> 00:03:20.301
Then context.

00:03:20.601 --> 00:03:23.389
The end of the task carries the clutter of the start.

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:24.925
That's context rot.

00:03:25.575 --> 00:03:26.521
And maintenance.

00:03:26.821 --> 00:03:32.532
Changing how review works means editing the prompt that also researches and writes.

00:03:32.832 --> 00:03:34.903
And each change affects everything.

00:03:35.553 --> 00:03:38.373
Picture the same pattern in an online store.

00:03:38.673 --> 00:03:44.562
An agent that answers delivery questions, looks up the order, and issues refunds.

00:03:44.862 --> 00:03:49.509
A simple where is my package still has the refund tool on the list.

00:03:49.809 --> 00:03:55.783
Text in a product description, or a creative user, pushes the model toward the side

00:03:55.783 --> 00:03:56.287
effect.

00:03:57.487 --> 00:03:58.730
Here's one for you.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.211
The content agent researches, writes and publishes.

00:04:02.511 --> 00:04:04.583
You can make one change this week.

00:04:04.883 --> 00:04:07.052
Which cut reduces the most risk?

00:04:10.102 --> 00:04:14.766
Take the publish tool out of the model's loop, and publish in a code step, with

00:04:14.766 --> 00:04:15.297
approval.

00:04:15.597 --> 00:04:20.392
A side effect tool available during research is the risk that already broke on

00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:20.822
Friday.

00:04:21.122 --> 00:04:23.843
Isolating the effect is the smallest cut.

00:04:24.143 --> 00:04:27.276
Research and writing can wait for the next slice.

00:04:27.926 --> 00:04:33.160
Adding a second role whose only job is to review rigorously, in the same context

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.646
with the same tools, isolates nothing.

00:04:35.946 --> 00:04:39.117
The reviewer still sees the pages, and can still publish.

00:04:39.417 --> 00:04:44.482
And telling the agent to forget the pages after the draft doesn't remove a single

00:04:44.482 --> 00:04:45.983
token, or a single tool.

00:04:47.183 --> 00:04:49.208
There are two refactorings.

00:04:49.508 --> 00:04:54.576
Extract subagent, when there are steps with their own tools and context, like

00:04:54.576 --> 00:04:55.893
research and review.

00:04:56.193 --> 00:04:59.969
Each becomes a subagent that returns only its result.

00:05:00.269 --> 00:05:05.332
And introduce a router, when the agent handles different kinds of requests, each

00:05:05.332 --> 00:05:06.851
needing different tools.

00:05:07.151 --> 00:05:12.381
Often, the final step, publish, pay or send, doesn't even need a model.

00:05:12.681 --> 00:05:15.143
It becomes code, behind approval.

00:05:15.793 --> 00:05:19.040
Here's the smallest cut that already reduces harm.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:24.645
Research reads the web in an isolated context, and returns cited facts.

00:05:24.945 --> 00:05:26.293
Not raw pages.

00:05:26.943 --> 00:05:30.786
Writing gets the brief plus those facts, without the pages.

00:05:31.436 --> 00:05:35.296
Review gets the draft plus the brand guide, without the writer's reasoning.

00:05:35.946 --> 00:05:41.425
And publishing is code, with a person in the loop, if the channel is public.

00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:43.473
But don't overcorrect.

00:05:43.773 --> 00:05:45.584
The opposite is a smell too.

00:05:45.884 --> 00:05:48.660
A pipeline of five agents with job titles.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.677
A researcher, an analyst, a writer, a reviewer, and an editor.

00:05:53.977 --> 00:05:58.360
Each takes the previous one's text, and returns nearly the same text.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:03.290
It costs five times as much, with no real split of tools or context.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:07.756
That smell has a name of its own, decorative multi-agent.

00:06:08.056 --> 00:06:12.773
Split where the nature of the work changes, not where the org chart wants a job

00:06:12.773 --> 00:06:13.131
title.

00:06:14.331 --> 00:06:16.137
Three cases to sharpen the eye.

00:06:16.437 --> 00:06:21.958
A support agent answers questions, looks up orders, and issues refunds, with one

00:06:21.958 --> 00:06:23.891
prompt and one set of tools.

00:06:24.191 --> 00:06:28.135
Refunds above a hundred dollars go to a person.

00:06:28.435 --> 00:06:29.945
Is it a mega agent?

00:06:30.245 --> 00:06:30.747
Yes.

00:06:31.047 --> 00:06:35.608
The approval covers big amounts, but the question answering path still has the

00:06:35.608 --> 00:06:37.069
refund tool within reach.

00:06:37.369 --> 00:06:39.658
A router would separate the paths.

00:06:40.308 --> 00:06:44.128
A research agent reads sixty pages and writes a report.

00:06:44.428 --> 00:06:45.947
No side effects at all.

00:06:46.247 --> 00:06:50.109
But the report sometimes ignores sources read early on.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:53.872
That's a milder version, mixed with context rot.

00:06:54.172 --> 00:06:58.279
Split the reading into subagents that return cited summaries.

00:06:58.929 --> 00:07:04.711
And an engineer using a terminal assistant to edit a repository, approving every

00:07:04.711 --> 00:07:06.084
change it proposes.

00:07:06.384 --> 00:07:10.291
Read and write tools in one agent, under continuous supervision.

00:07:10.591 --> 00:07:11.214
That's fine.

00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:16.751
The smell hurts when the same loop reads the web, and fires a side effect, with

00:07:16.751 --> 00:07:17.811
nobody watching.

00:07:19.011 --> 00:07:21.090
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:27.777
When one agent researches, writes, reviews and acts, every step inherits every risk.

00:07:28.077 --> 00:07:33.303
Separate the reading from the acting, give review fresh eyes, and turn the final

00:07:33.303 --> 00:07:35.655
side effect into code with approval.

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:38.419
That's usually the cut that matters most.

00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:44.091
In the next lesson, we'll make the main cut step by step: extract subagent.

00:07:44.391 --> 00:07:45.210
See you there.
